        "title": "Animal Diversity and Phylogeny",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Animal phylogeny has been transformed by molecular data. The deep topology — Bilateria splitting into Protostomia and Deuterostomia; Ecdysozoa and Lophotrochozoa within Protostomia — was largely unknown before 1997 (Aguinaldo et al.). Most \"classic\" animal relationships based on morphology were wrong. The molecular tree is now well-resolved at the phylum level; within-phylum relationships and species-level systematics remain active research."
    }
